To output a PDF in SAS with a journal format, you can use the ODS PDF statement along with the JOURNAL subroutine of the Proc Report procedure. Here's an example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=Journal;
proc report data=sashelp.cars nowd;
  columns Make Model Type LS_desc;
  define Make / group;
  define Model / across;
  define Type / "Type": pseudo.;
  define LS_desc / analysis variable label='Length (in.)';
  run;
ods pdf close;
```
In this example, the ODS PDF statement specifies the file name and style (in this case, "Journal"). The Proc Report procedure is used to create a report, and the JOURNAL subroutine is specified using the STYLE= option.

Inside the Proc Report procedure, you can define the columns and layout of your report using the DEFINE statements. In this example, we're using a group by variable (Make), a across variable (Model), and a pseudo variable (Type) to create a table with three columns.

The output PDF file will have a journal-style layout with a header, footer, and table format that is similar to a printed journal.

You can customize the appearance of the PDF file by using various options available with the ODS PDF statement, such as:

* STYLE= option: Choose from various pre-defined styles, including Journal, Newspaper, and Tech.
* BODY=my_style: Define your own custom style for the body of the PDF.
* FOOT=my_style: Define your own custom style for the footer of the PDF.
* HEAD=my_style: Define your own custom style for the header of the PDF.
* STARTXTXT=my_text: Specify the text to display at the start of the PDF.

