Here is a simple C++ program to control the GPIO pin 12 on a Raspberry Pi 4B using the pigpio library:

**Note:** Before running this program, make sure you have installed the pigpio library on your Raspberry Pi and have set up the GPIO pins according to the pigpio library's documentation.

**pigpio Library Installation:**
```
sudo apt-get install pigpio
```
**Program:**

```cpp
#include <pigpio.h>

#define PIN 12 // GPIO pin 12

int main() {
    // Initialize the GPIO library
    if (gpioInitialise() < 0) {
        cout << "pigpio initialization failed" << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Set pin 12 as an output
    gpioSetup(PIN, PI_OUTPUT);

    // Set pin 12 high (1)
    gpioWrite(PIN, PI_HIGH);

    cout << "Pin 12 is high" << endl;

    // Set pin 12 low (0)
    gpioWrite(PIN, PI_LOW);

    cout << "Pin 12 is low" << endl;

    // Clean up
    gpioTerminate();

    return 0;
}
```

